1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
One sky. Sardinian shepherds saw — a
stone hut.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:10,000
Sardinia, west of Italy, is the
Mediterranean’s second-largest island.
Sheep outnumber people about two to one.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:17,000
These star names are in the dialect of
Sedilo, a small town in the island’s
centre.

4
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:24,000
One book reads them as island life: a
shepherd’s hut, a winegrower’s grapes, a
farmer’s sickle.

5
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:32,000
Taurus’s V and Aldebaran: Su Pinnetu, a
shepherd’s hut. Stone walls, a pointed
roof of branches.

6
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:40,000
The Pleiades are S’Udrone: a big bunch
of grapes. Romanians see a hen and
chicks; Sardinia sees grapes.

7
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:48,000
Below, Orion is Sos Bacheddos: the
shepherds’ sticks. The Belt has its own
name: Sas Tres Marias, the Three Marias.

8
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:05,000
A shepherds’ clock: on summer nights the
flock grazes until the sticks rise.
Then, back to the fold.

9
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:13,000
Auriga is Sa Mandra, the sheepfold.
Greeks saw a charioteer with a goat;
Sardinians, a fence for sheep.

10
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:21,000
Leo’s backward question mark is Sa
Frache: the farmer’s sickle. This week
after dark, look high in the southwest.

11
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:24,000
Paris, 1786: a star map of lines.

12
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:27,000
Follow for a new sky every week.

